## 📚 레퍼런스 (또는 새로 알게 된 내용) 혹은 궁금한 사항들
[가장 쉽게 느낀 점]
Vue는 React의 JSX 방식과 달리 HTML, CSS, JavaScript를 구분해서 작성할 수 있어 코드 구조가 직관적이고 이해하기 쉬웠다. 또한 React에서 JS 문법으로 처리하던 조건부 렌더링이나 반복문을 Vue에서는 `v-if`, `v-for` 등의 디렉티브로 간단하게 구현할 수 있어 편리한 것 같다.

[가장 어렵게 느낀 점]
React의 JSX 방식에 익숙해져 있어 Vue의 템플릿 문법과 디렉티브 사용 방식이 어렵게 느껴졌다. 특히 React의 `useState`, `useEffect`와 Vue의 `ref`, `computed`, `watch` 등 반응형 데이터 처리 방식의 차이를 비교하고 이해하는 것이 어려웠다.

<br />

## 📝 Vue.js 문법 정리
### 1. SFC (Single-File Component)

`.vue` 파일 하나에 세 가지를 함께 작성하는 구조

```
<script setup>
// import, 변수, 함수, props
</script>

<template>
<!-- HTML -->
</template>

<style scoped>
/* CSS (scoped = 이 컴포넌트에만 적용) */
</style>
```

<br />

### 2. 템플릿 문법

**📌 보간법**

데이터를 화면에 출력하는 문법. JSX의 `{ }`와 동일한 역할.

```html
<p>{{ message }}</p>
```

<br />

**📌 디렉티브**

v-로 시작하는 특수 속성. React의 JS 문법(`&&`, `map`, `onClick`)을 하나의 속성으로 선언하는 방식.

- `v-if` / `v-else-if` / `v-else` : 조건부 렌더링
- `v-for` : 반복 렌더링(:key 사용)

```html
<!-- 조건 -->
<template v-if="isLoggedIn">
  <p>로그인 성공</p>
</template>

<!-- 반복 -->
<li v-for="todo in todos" :key="todo.id">{{ todo.text }}</li>
```

<br />

**📌  바인딩 문법**

속성이나 props를 동적으로 전달. `v-bind(:)`

```html
<!-- 축약형 -->
<img :src="imageUrl">

<!-- 컴포넌트에 props 전달 -->
<Greeting :name="userName" />
```

<br />

**📌 이벤트 핸들링**

이벤트를 부여하여 동작을 발생시킴. `v-on(@)`

```html
<!-- 클릭 이벤트 -->
<button @click="sayHello">인사하기</button>

<button @click="remove(id)">삭제</button>
```

<br />

**📌 `v-if` vs `v-show`**

- v-if

## What
User feedback: the product page's reviews were display-only. A new client block, `MyReview`, now sits under "Ratings & reviews", above the public list.

| State | What the customer sees |
|---|---|
| **Signed out** | "Bought this? Rate & review it" with **Sign in to review**, which links to `/account/login?next=<this product>`. No ratings call is made. |
| **Signed in, no review yet** | A **Rate & review** button opens the form: a 1–5 star picker (a real radio group with labels, arrow keys and a 44px target per star), an optional comment (1000 chars with a counter) and a privacy note. Saving needs a star; the comment is sent trimmed, or `null` if blank. |
| **Signed in, has a review** | **Your review** card with stars, comment, a *Verified purchase* badge, *Awaiting approval* / *Not published* chips and any DRS reply. It has **Edit review** (the form starts from the saved review) and **Delete** (confirmed in a dialog). The same review is hidden from the public list below, so it shows once. |
| **Not eligible (never bought it)** | See below. |

### How eligibility actually works
I read `drs-backend/app/api/v1/products.py` (`rate_product`) and the mobile M5 flow (`drs-mobile/.../review_editor_screen.dart`):
- **The backend doesn't refuse non-buyers.** Any signed-in user can rate.
- **A verified purchase publishes at once:** a paid order, or a delivered item containing the product.
- **Anyone else's review is saved as `pending`** and held for moderation. Editing a rejected review sends it back to `pending`.
- **The app treats it the same way,** so the web mirrors it:
  - the form says up front that "Reviews from customers who haven't bought this product are checked before they appear";
  - saving shows "Thanks! Your review will appear once it's approved";
  - the card shows *Awaiting approval* and "Only you can see it until our team approves it".

There's no endpoint that answers "may I review?" ahead of time. The customer's own review is found in the signed-in `GET /products/{id}/ratings` response, which includes it in any status with `mine: true`.

Other API mapping:
- create and update both use `POST /products/{id}/ratings` (`rate_product`, which updates the existing review if there is one);
- delete uses `DELETE /products/{id}/ratings` (`delete_my_rating`);
- a 401 while saving sends the customer to sign in, and other errors show the API's `detail`.

## Mock
The mock now implements those backend rules:
- one review per user per product;
- a verified purchase publishes; anyone else's review is `pending`;
- editing a rejected review re-queues it;
- a retired product gets a 404;
- the public list hides held reviews from everyone but their author.

The mock customer has a delivered, paid order of product 1 (verified) and has never bought product 3 (held for approval).

## Tests
- **Unit (`tests/unit/reviews.test.tsx`, 9):**
  - validation, the payload, the save messages and the status chips;
  - the component, for signed out, submit (a star is required, the comment trimmed), held (unverified), edit with cancel and update, delete, and an API error.
- **e2e (`e2e/reviews.spec.ts`, 4 tests × 4 viewports):**
  - signed-out prompt and its redirect;
  - submit → reload persists → edit (pre-filled) → delete (dialog) → reload, on a verified purchase;
  - an unverified review is held, labelled, and absent from the public list;
  - picking stars from the keyboard.
  - Every page also gets axe and the no-h-scroll check.
- **Local runs:**
  - typecheck, lint and vitest (239) pass;
  - the reviews e2e passes (16);
  - the PDP-touching catalog, polish, qa, security and engagement e2e at 360/768/1024/1440 pass (92).

